      "frozen_source_lines": [
        {
          "line": 41,
          "text": "**Given:** the Axiom of Choice; a coefficient field $k$; a smooth complex algebraic variety $M$ of pure dimension $d$ with its analytic topology; morphisms of complex algebraic varieties $q:A\\to M$ and $u:B\\to M$; the fiber product $T=A\\times_MB$ with its projections $a:T\\to A$, $b:T\\to B$; the diagonal $\\Delta:M\\to M\\times M$; the map $F=(q,u):A\\times B\\to M\\times M$; and the closed embedding $\\theta:T\\hookrightarrow A\\times B$ of the support, which realizes $T$ as $F^{-1}(\\Delta(M))$. For the equivariant assertions, additionally impose the characteristic-zero, product-general-linear-group and equivariance hypotheses of [F3]."
        },
        {
          "line": 61,
          "text": "2.1 Pullback and cap product on arbitrary supports. Put $S=A\\times B$. Proper base change along $F=(q,u)$ identifies $F^*\\Delta_*k_M[-2d]$ with $\\theta_*k_T[-2d]$, so $F^*g_\\Delta$ gives $g_F:\\theta_*k_T[-2d]\\to k_S$. For a dualizing class $c:k_S\\to\\omega_S[-n]$, compose $c\\circ g_F$ and use $\\theta_*\\dashv\\theta^!$ and $\\theta^!\\omega_S=\\omega_T$. The resulting map $k_T\\to\\omega_T[2d-n]$ is a Borel\u2013Moore class of degree $n-2d$ on $T$. This defines the supported cap operation even if $T$ is singular or has excess dimension; no purity of $\\theta$ is asserted. The external product uses $\\omega_A\\boxtimes\\omega_B=\\omega_S$: on the supplied finite local product cochain models, compact-support cochains tensor, and their finite-dimensional duals tensor as well, giving this canonical comparison and its restriction compatibility. [F1, F2, F4, step 1.1, construct]"
        },
        {
          "line": 63,
          "text": "3.1 Comparison with the exchange mate. For a constructible $C$ on $M$, the mate is the adjoint of $b_!a^*q^!C\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{BC}^{-1}}u^*q_!q^!C\\xrightarrow{u^*\\epsilon_q}u^*C$, where the arbitrary-map proper-support exchange of [F1] supplies $\\mathrm{BC}:u^*q_!\\xrightarrow{\\sim}b_!a^*$ and $\\epsilon_q$ is the exceptional counit. To identify its dualizing evaluation with step 2.1, factor $q$ as its graph $\\Gamma_q:A\\hookrightarrow A\\times M$ followed by the second projection. The graph is the pullback of $\\Delta$ along $(q,\\operatorname{id}_M):A\\times M\\to M\\times M$; its supported orientation map is that pullback of $g_\\Delta$. Base change of the graph along $\\operatorname{id}_A\\times u$ is exactly $\\theta$, so its supported orientation map after this second pullback is $g_F$. For the product projection, tensor\u2013Hom evaluation with the relative dualizing factor $\\omega_A$ computes the counit; this is the finite product cochain evaluation of step 2.1. Composing the graph counit and that evaluation gives $\\epsilon_q$ by the composition-of-counits identity in [F1]. Cartesian pasting therefore makes its base-changed composite exactly $u^*\\epsilon_q\\circ\\mathrm{BC}^{-1}$ above. Taking adjoints and using $q^!k_M=\\omega_A[-2d]$ proves that the exchange, paired with the second dualizing class by tensor evaluation, is $c\\circ g_F$ under $\\theta_*\\dashv\\theta^!$. Every map retains support on $T$ throughout this factorization. [F1, F2, step 1.1, step 2.1]"
